Her work on producing nanocellulose from local biomass positioned Malaysia at the forefront of nanomaterials research, offering a green alternative to petroleum-based materials. Her work had supported the global transition toward circular and bio-based economies. Her innovations earned two Bioeconomy Innovation Awards at BioMalaysia 2017 for Most Commercial Ready Product and Most Innovative Product. These innovations resulted in six patents, two copyrights, and the successful commercialisation of two technologies. Professor Ts Dr Hidayah has further licensed her nanocellulose technology to ZoepNano Sdn. Bhd., Malaysia’s first nanocellulose-based company, co-founded by her and her students, as well as Nextgreen Pulp and Paper Sdn. Bhd.

Beyond her scientific achievements, she had made significant contributions to Malaysia’s science and technology policy landscape. She served as an expert contributor to key national frameworks, including the Selangor Biotechnology Action Plan (2021–2030), National Nanotechnology Roadmap (2021–2030), National Advanced Materials Technology Roadmap (2021–2030), Malaysia Plastics Sustainability Roadmap (2021–2030), and National Biomass Action Plan (2023–2030). In addition, she has been an active member of the ASM Science Journal Editorial Board since 2022.

At the international level, she had established strong collaborations with Mitsubishi Heavy Industries Japan, Nagase Singapore Pte. Ltd., and SCG Packaging PCL in Thailand, promoting knowledge transfer and industrial partnerships. She also represented Malaysia at numerous international platforms, delivering invited lectures and research presentations in Japan, Korea, Australia, Canada, China, and India. Her leadership extends globally as Secretary of the Nanomaterial Production Subcommittee under the TAPPI Nano Division, and as a member of several international scientific committees and editorial boards.', 'Bioprocess Engineering > Biomass Technology > Biopolymers > Nanocellulose', 'BEng in Engineering (Process and Food), Universiti Putra Malaysia, 2004
